  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Flooding Looking to be less of a threat across\nSouthwestern PA this afternoon based on the latest high \nresolution model guidance.\n\nWe're seeing a midday lull in the heavier showers across the\nregion under the Flood Watch (i.e., Somerset County), and \nlatest HRRR guidance indicates that the next batch of heavier \nconvection over Central Ohio will will get eroded/shunted just\nto the south of the Laurel Highlands during the mid-late\nafternoon hours as a cold front slips SE from Nrn Ohio and NW\nPA. \n\nBest SFC Based CAPE over 1000 j/kg (via the HREF) fueling low-\ntopped TSRA will also stay confined just to the south of the\nMason/Dixon Line. \n\nThat being said, will monitor these aforementioned trends\nclosely and leave the Flood Watch up for the next several hours\n(til its expiration at 20Z) or possibly cancel it early. \n\nThe rest of the forecast is pretty much on track with\npredominantly dry conditions across Central and Northern PA and\nafternoon high temps ranging from a few deg below normal over\nthe Laurel Highlands, to as much as 8-10 deg F above normal over\nthe NE (where the most sun will occur \"ahead\" of the cold front. \n\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Seasonably cooler and tranquil/pleasant weather \nconditions forecast Thursday through the last weekend of May \n\nA mainly-dry cold front delivers cooler and drier/less humid air\nfor Thursday followed by high pressure through late week. \nReinforcing troughing over the Northeast should result in near \nto below normal temperatures (seasonably cool) and low \nprecipitation chances for the last weekend of May.",
